[[Poland]] ({{langx|pl|Polska}}) is a country that extends across the [[North European Plain]] from the [[Sudetes]] and [[Carpathian Mountains]] in the south to the sandy beaches of the [[Baltic Sea]] in the [[north]]. Poland is the fifth-most populous country of the [[European Union]] and the ninth-largest country in [[Europe]] by area. The territory of Poland covers approximately {{convert|312696|km2|sqmi|0|abbr=on}}, of which 98.52% is land and 1.48% is water.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://stat.gov.pl/obszary-tematyczne/ludnosc/ludnosc/powierzchnia-i-ludnosc-w-przekroju-terytorialnym-w-2018-roku,7,15.html|title=Powierzchnia i ludność w przekroju terytorialnym w 2018 roku|website=stat.gov.pl}}</ref> The Polish coastline was estimated at {{convert|770|km|mi|0|abbr=on}} in length.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.paih.gov.pl/polska_w_liczbach/terytorium|title=PAIH &#124; Terytorium|website=www.paih.gov.pl|access-date=1 November 2020|archive-date=30 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210730092455/https://www.paih.gov.pl/polska_w_liczbach/terytorium|url-status=dead}}</ref> Poland's highest point is [[Mount Rysy|Rysy]], at {{convert|2500|m|ft|0|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Wysokość szczytów w Tatrach do poprawki. Wyższe okazały się m.in. Rysy, Świnica czy Skrajny Granat |url=https://www.pap.pl/aktualnosci/news,1412132,wysokosc-szczytow-w-tatrach-do-poprawki-wyzsze-okazaly-sie-min-rysy |access-date=2023-11-19 |website=PAP - Polish Press Agency}}</ref>

Geographically, Poland is a diverse country; although most of the central terrain is flat, there is an abundance of lakes, rivers, hills, swamps, beaches, islands and forests elsewhere. The Baltic coast has two natural [[harbour]]s, the larger situated in the [[Gdańsk]]-[[Gdynia]] region, and the smaller near [[Szczecin]] in the far northwest. The northeastern region, also known as the [[Masurian Lake District]] with 1,300 lakes,<ref name="mazury.info">[http://mazury.info.pl/ Masurian Lake District, at mazury.info.pl]&nbsp;{{in lang|pl}}</ref> is densely wooded and sparsely populated. To the south of the lake district, and across central Poland a vast region of plains stretches all the way to the [[Sudeten mountains|Sudetes]] on the [[Czech Republic|Czech]] and [[Germany|German]] borders southwest, and to the [[Carpathian Mountains|Carpathians]] on the Czech, [[Slovakia|Slovak]] and [[Ukraine|Ukrainian]] borders southeast. The central lowlands had been formed by [[Glacier#Glacial deposits|glacial erosion]] in the [[Pleistocene]] [[ice age]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Wpływ zlodowaceń na rzeźbę Polski - Zintegrowana Platforma Edukacyjna |url=https://zpe.gov.pl/a/wplyw-zlodowacen-na-rzezbe-polski/D16rvISjX |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220814001030/https://zpe.gov.pl/a/wplyw-zlodowacen-na-rzezbe-polski/D16rvISjX |archive-date=2022-08-14 |access-date=2023-11-19 |website=zpe.gov.pl}}</ref>

The country's longest and most prominent river is the [[Vistula]] at {{convert|1047|km|0|abbr=off}} in length, also the ninth-longest in Europe.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://pomorskie.travel/en/Discover-Cultural_heritage-Others/5502/Wis_a|title= Vistula River|website= pomorskie.travel|access-date= 13 August 2018|quote= Vistula - the most important and the longest river in Poland, and the largest river in the area of the Baltic Sea. The length of Vistula is 1047 km.|archive-date= 13 August 2018|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180813074958/http://pomorskie.travel/en/Discover-Cultural_heritage-Others/5502/Wis_a|url-status= dead}}</ref> Other notable rivers within the administrative borders are [[Warta]] at {{convert|808|km|mi|abbr=on}}, and the [[Oder]] at {{convert|741|km|mi|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-02-24 |title=Najdłuższe rzeki w Polsce. Która rzeka w Polsce jest najdłuższa? |url=https://podroze.gazeta.pl/podroze/7,114158,27636738,najdluzsze-rzeki-w-polsce-ktora-rzeka-w-polsce-jest-najdluzsza.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230607223312/https://podroze.gazeta.pl/podroze/7,114158,27636738,najdluzsze-rzeki-w-polsce-ktora-rzeka-w-polsce-jest-najdluzsza.html |archive-date=2023-06-07 |access-date=2023-11-19 |website=gazetapl |language=pl}}</ref> Poland's largest lake is [[Śniardwy]] with the surface area of {{convert|113.4|km2|mi2|abbr=on}}, followed by [[Mamry]] with {{convert|102.8|km2|mi2|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Największe jeziora w Polsce |url=https://www.naukowiec.org/tablice/geografia/najwieksze-jeziora-w-polsce_796.html |access-date=2023-11-19 |website=Naukowiec.org}}</ref>

{{Short description|Imperial Chinese government organ}}
{{hatnote|This article is about the ''Yuefu'' governmental Music Bureau. For the related poetry genre, please see ''[[yuefu]]''.}}

The '''Music Bureau''' ([[Traditional Chinese character|Traditional Chinese]]: 樂府; [[Simplified Chinese character|Simplified Chinese]]: 乐府; [[Pinyin|Hanyu Pinyin]]: ''yuèfǔ'', and sometimes known as the "'''Imperial Music Bureau'''") served in the capacity of an organ of various imperial government bureaucracies of China: discontinuously and in various incarnations, the Music Bureau was charged directly, by the emperor (or other monarchical ruler), or indirectly, through the royal (or imperial) government to perform various tasks related to music, poetry, entertainment, or religious worship. These tasks included both musical and lyrical research and development, and also directing performances.

The existence of a Music Bureau was typical of various [[Chinese dynasties]], though the Music Bureau's prominence and influence may have peaked during the reign of Han dynasty emperor [[Emperor Wu of Han|Wu]], who was especially interested in such activities and engaged himself accordingly. The mission of the Music Bureau was concerned with poetry, music, and their live performances, because these concepts were not really considered to be distinct: lyrics are a part of music, and traditional Chinese poems, particularly ''[[shi (poetry)|shi]]'', were considered to be suitable for chanting or singing, and poetic verses in accompaniment with instrumental music were often presented together as integral parts of performances. In the case of the Music Bureau, performances were oriented towards religious rites or entertainment in service to the emperor.

The songs/poems collected or developed by the Han music bureau received the appellation of "''yuefu''"; but, eventually, the term "Music Bureau", or ''yuefu'' (also ''yueh-fu'') also came to be applied to a category of [[Classical Chinese poetry]] which was based upon the standard forms and themes documented or promoted by the Music Bureau staff during the [[Han dynasty]]. Known as ''[[yuefu]]'' (meaning "in the style of the Music Bureau poetry"), this type of poetry made major contributions to [[Han poetry]], as well as the [[Jian'an poetry]] of the late Han and early [[Six Dynasties]]. There was also an important later [[Tang poetry]] literary revival of the ''yuefu'' poetic forms.

